#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:1
msgid "Garbage Collection"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:3
msgid ""
"`nix-env` operations such as upgrades (`-u`) and uninstall (`-e`) never "
"actually delete packages from the system. All they do (as shown above) is to "
"create a new user environment that no longer contains symlinks to the "
"“deleted” packages."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:8
msgid ""
"Of course, since disk space is not infinite, unused packages should be "
"removed at some point. You can do this by running the Nix garbage collector. "
"It will remove from the Nix store any package not used (directly or "
"indirectly) by any generation of any profile."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:13
msgid ""
"Note however that as long as old generations reference a package, it will "
"not be deleted. After all, we wouldn’t be able to do a rollback otherwise. "
"So in order for garbage collection to be effective, you should also delete "
"(some) old generations. Of course, this should only be done if you are "
"certain that you will not need to roll back."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:19
msgid "To delete all old (non-current) generations of your current profile:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:25
msgid "Instead of `old` you can also specify a list of generations, e.g.,"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:31
msgid ""
"To delete all generations older than a specified number of days (except the "
"current generation), use the `d` suffix. For example,"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:38
msgid "deletes all generations older than two weeks."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:40
msgid ""
"After removing appropriate old generations you can run the garbage collector "
"as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:47
msgid ""
"The behaviour of the garbage collector is affected by the `keep-derivations` "
"(default: true) and `keep-outputs` (default: false) options in the Nix "
"configuration file. The defaults will ensure that all derivations that are "
"build-time dependencies of garbage collector roots will be kept and that all "
"output paths that are runtime dependencies will be kept as well. All other "
"derivations or paths will be collected. (This is usually what you want, but "
"while you are developing it may make sense to keep outputs to ensure that "
"rebuild times are quick.) If you are feeling uncertain, you can also first "
"view what files would be deleted:"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:62
msgid ""
"Likewise, the option `--print-live` will show the paths that _won’t_ be "
"deleted."
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:65
msgid ""
"There is also a convenient little utility `nix-collect-garbage`, which when "
"invoked with the `-d` (`--delete-old`) switch deletes all old generations of "
"all profiles in `/nix/var/nix/profiles`. So"
msgstr ""

#: source/package-management/garbage-collection.md:73
msgid "is a quick and easy way to clean up your system."
msgstr ""
